# MUV CONSTRUCTION: BUILDING THE CHASSIS & INTEGRATING THE COMPONENTS

The MUV began with a simple objective: create a small electric vehicle that could carry a person and be adapted to perform a variety 
of utility tasks. The construction evolved through experimentation, practical decisions, and a willingness to modify things as I learned what worked.

What follows is an overview of how the current MUV is constructed. It is not intended to be a dimensioned blueprint or a final set of 
building instructions. It documents the construction approach, the materials, and the solutions that have made the current prototype functional.

## 1. THE MAIN CHASSIS

The main chassis is constructed from larger-size perforated square steel tubing. Each side of the chassis begins with a single, unaltered 
four-foot length of this tubing. These two lengths form the primary upper side members.

A lower support structure is made from additional square tubing. Each lower support is cut and bent at four points to create a dropped profile. 
This altered piece supports the unmodified four-foot side member, and the two are bolted together.

This approach provides the basic side structure while creating a lower area for mounting and supporting the vehicle's major components.

The left and right side assemblies are joined using four short sections of the same larger-size square tubing. These sections act as frame 
stabilizers, with two positioned beneath the side assemblies. Together, they establish the width of the chassis and provide the openings and 
support needed for the battery and other components.

A wooden panel is added to the lower supports to create the battery platform. The 48V battery rests on this panel within the space formed by 
the side members and frame stabilizers.

## 2. THE REAR DRIVE ASSEMBLY

The rear drive wheel is mounted using the smaller-size perforated square tubing.

Sections of this smaller tubing are attached to the rear wheel assembly and slide into the larger square tubing used in the lower chassis structure. 
This creates a removable rear-wheel mounting arrangement.

The arrangement has proven particularly useful. The rear wheel assembly can be removed without taking apart the main chassis. I have removed and 
reinstalled it several times, and the approach has worked very well.

This is one of the features I consider especially successful: designing the vehicle so that major components can be serviced or replaced without 
dismantling everything around them.

## 3. THE FRONT STRUCTURE & GLOVEBOX

A similar use of the smaller square tubing creates the front portion of the chassis.

Two altered sections of the smaller tubing are inserted into the larger side members. These pieces establish the structure in front of the rider 
and help form the space used for the glove box.

The front sections connect to the remaining two frame stabilizers. These connections add rigidity to the overall chassis and provide the mounting 
structure for the front forks.

The front forks are secured to the frame stabilizers using two three-inch U-bolts. Shims can be added where needed to improve the fit between the 
fork assembly and the mounting structure.

The front wheel is smaller than the conventional size that might otherwise be used with these forks. The geometry of the vehicle does not allow 
for the expected wheel size. In practice, this compromise has not negatively affected the riding experience.

## 4. SEAT & UPPER FRAME BRACING

The seat is constructed from wood with a foam-covered upper surface.

Two pieces of perforated angle iron run along the upper portion of the chassis. They provide additional bracing while also creating a solid 
mounting arrangement for the seat.

The seat is positioned between these two angle-iron members. It serves two purposes: supporting the rider and acting as a cover over the battery area.

This arrangement makes use of the available space while keeping the battery protected beneath the seating area.

## 5. MOTOR CONTROLLER & ELECTRICAL LAYOUT

The motor controller is located in the lower portion of the chassis, beneath the glove box.

Its electrical connections face rearward, toward the battery. This keeps the controller's connection side oriented toward the primary power 
source and places it within the protected interior of the chassis.

The battery, controller, and associated wiring are concealed behind the side panels.

## 6. SIDE PANELS & ENVIRONMENTAL PROTECTION

The side panels are made from 1/4-inch luan plywood.

Their purpose is not simply cosmetic. They conceal the battery, motor controller, and wiring while providing a degree of protection from dirt, 
moisture, and debris kicked up during riding.

The panels help turn the open structural chassis into a more enclosed vehicle while retaining the relatively simple construction approach used 
throughout the project.

## 7. THE GLOVEBOX, ACCESS FLOOR & STORAGE

The glove box is more than a storage compartment. It also provides access to several items that need to remain available while using the vehicle.

Its floor consists of two pieces of 1/4-inch plywood connected by two hinges. This arrangement allows the driver to remove the floor or raise the 
front section and slide the other section forward.

Opening the floor provides access to the battery charger, charging plug, and tool kit. The tool kit currently includes wrenches, Allen keys, and 
an air pump.

There is another useful feature built into this arrangement. When the front section is raised and the two hinged pieces are moved forward, they 
create a slot that can hold a Bluetooth speaker. The speaker can then be used with a media player while riding.

It is a small example of how the vehicle has evolved through actual use. The space serves several purposes rather than being dedicated to just one.

## 8. THE KICKSTAND

The kickstand is a simple, improvised assembly.

It is made from a truncated triangular arrangement of threaded rod that fits into holes in the chassis near the front of the vehicle.

A scrap piece of square tubing is bolted to one side of the assembly. This acts as a stop, preventing the kickstand from rotating too far forward 
and allowing the vehicle to topple.

It is a straightforward solution made from readily available materials, and it does the job.

## 9. FOOTRESTS AND RIDER COMFORT

The lower front portion of the chassis also provides the rider's footrests.

These are made from sections of square tubing and are positioned to give the rider a comfortable place to rest their feet while operating the vehicle.

The overall riding position, combined with the seat and footrest placement, has made the MUV comfortable to operate.

## 10. WHAT HAS THE CONSTRUCTION PROVEN?

The current prototype has now accumulated more than 810 miles.

During that time, it has required almost no maintenance apart from the modifications and improvements I have made along the way.

The vehicle currently carries my 200-pound weight along with my 56-pound dog and moves us without difficulty across its operating speed range, 
from a crawl to its higher speed settings.

It can move along at approximately 2 mph when a slow, controlled pace is needed. Its walk function provides approximately 4–5 mph on level ground. 
It can also enter and exit moderate ditches with ease.

It has become a genuinely useful vehicle rather than simply an experimental project sitting in a workshop.

## 11. A CONSTRUCTION APPROACH THAT CAN EVOLVE

One of the most important lessons from building the MUV is that the construction does not have to be perfect on the first attempt.

The use of bolted connections, nested square tubing, removable assemblies, and accessible components has made it possible to change things without starting over.

The rear-wheel mounting arrangement is a good example. The wheel can be removed independently of the chassis. The glove box provides access to equipment and tools. 
The side panels can be removed to reach the electrical components.

These are practical construction decisions, but they also point toward the larger idea behind the MUV: a vehicle that can be modified, repaired, 
and adapted as its purpose changes.

The current version is working, and I have put more than 810 miles on it. But I don't consider it finished. There are still improvements to make, 
ideas to explore, and configurations that have yet to be developed.

That is part of the point.

The MUV is not just about building one small electric vehicle. It is about developing a construction approach that makes the vehicle accessible to 
modification and opens the door to other possibilities.
